          <div class="info-title-group">
            <h3 class="info-dialog-title">Model Architecture</h3>
            <p class="info-dialog-subtitle">
              A compact multilingual Transformer built from scratch using a Pre-LN encoder–decoder architecture, shared subword representations, and an English-centric pivot translation strategy.
            </p>
          </div>
          <button class="icon-btn-subtle" id="btnCloseInfoModal" title="Close">
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"/><line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"/></svg>
          </button>
        </div>

        <div class="info-content-body">
          <!-- 8 Architecture Feature Cards Grid -->
          <div class="arch-cards-grid">
            <!-- 1. Transformer Encoder-Decoder -->
            <div class="arch-card">
              <div class="arch-card-header">
                <span class="arch-card-title">Transformer Encoder–Decoder</span>
              </div>
              <span class="arch-card-sub">6 Encoder + 6 Decoder Layers</span>
              <p class="arch-card-p">
                Built from scratch using a Transformer encoder–decoder architecture for multilingual sequence-to-sequence translation. The encoder learns contextual representations of the source sentence, while the decoder generates the target translation autoregressively.
              </p>
              <span class="arch-card-ref">Research basis: Vaswani et al. (2017), Attention Is All You Need.</span>
            </div>

            <!-- 2. Pre-Layer Normalization -->
            <div class="arch-card">
              <div class="arch-card-header">
                <span class="arch-card-title">Pre-Layer Normalization</span>
              </div>
              <span class="arch-card-sub">LayerNorm Before Each Sublayer</span>
              <p class="arch-card-p">
                The architecture uses Pre-Layer Normalization, applying LayerNorm before attention and feed-forward sublayers rather than after residual connections. This design was selected to improve optimization stability and gradient flow during from-scratch training.
              </p>
              <span class="arch-card-ref">Research basis: Xiong et al. (2020), On Layer Normalization in the Transformer Architecture.</span>
            </div>

            <!-- 3. Compact Attention Design -->
            <div class="arch-card">
              <div class="arch-card-header">
                <span class="arch-card-title">Compact Attention Design</span>
              </div>
              <span class="arch-card-sub">4 Attention Heads · d_model 512</span>
              <p class="arch-card-p">
                The model uses four attention heads to balance representational capacity and computational efficiency. Reducing attention complexity helps maintain a more appropriate model scale for limited training resources.
              </p>
              <span class="arch-card-ref">Design motivation: A smaller Transformer configuration was chosen instead of directly adopting large-scale WMT-style defaults.</span>
            </div>

            <!-- 4. Reduced Feed-Forward Network -->
            <div class="arch-card">
              <div class="arch-card-header">
                <span class="arch-card-title">Reduced Feed-Forward Network</span>
              </div>
              <span class="arch-card-sub">FFN Dimension: 1024</span>
              <p class="arch-card-p">
                The feed-forward dimension was reduced from the larger 2048-dimensional baseline to 1024, lowering parameter count and computational cost while keeping the core model representation dimension at 512.
              </p>
              <span class="arch-card-ref">Design motivation: Balances model capacity, dataset scale, and available training compute.</span>
            </div>

            <!-- 5. English-Centric Pivot Translation -->
            <div class="arch-card">
              <div class="arch-card-header">
                <span class="arch-card-title">English-Centric Pivot Translation</span>
              </div>
              <span class="arch-card-sub">English as a Shared Translation Hub</span>
              <p class="arch-card-p">
                Instead of training every possible language pair directly, the model learns translations between each supported language and English. Non-English translations are performed through English as an intermediate pivot.
              </p>
              <div class="hub-diagram">
                <span class="hub-node">Thai</span>
                <span style="font-size: 11px; color: #94a3b8;">➔</span>
                <span class="hub-node active">English (Pivot Hub)</span>
                <span style="font-size: 11px; color: #94a3b8;">➔</span>
                <span class="hub-node">Chinese / Others</span>
              </div>
              <span class="arch-card-ref">Related research: Johnson et al. (2017), Google's Multilingual Neural Machine Translation System.</span>
            </div>

            <!-- 6. Shared Multilingual Vocabulary -->
            <div class="arch-card">
              <div class="arch-card-header">
                <span class="arch-card-title">Shared Multilingual Vocabulary</span>
              </div>
              <span class="arch-card-sub">SentencePiece · 32K Shared Vocabulary</span>
              <p class="arch-card-p">
                A shared SentencePiece vocabulary is used across all supported languages, allowing the model to process different writing systems through a single tokenization pipeline without relying on language-specific whitespace segmentation.
              </p>
              <span class="arch-card-ref">Research basis: Kudo &amp; Richardson (2018), SentencePiece Subword Tokenizer.</span>
            </div>

            <!-- 7. Positional Encoding -->
            <div class="arch-card">
              <div class="arch-card-header">
                <span class="arch-card-title">Positional Encoding</span>
              </div>
              <span class="arch-card-sub">Sinusoidal Position Information</span>
              <p class="arch-card-p">
                Because Transformer layers do not inherently process tokens sequentially, positional encodings are added to token embeddings to provide information about token order while retaining parallel computation advantages.
              </p>
              <span class="arch-card-ref">Research basis: Vaswani et al. (2017).</span>
            </div>

            <!-- 8. Final Layer Normalization -->
            <div class="arch-card">
              <div class="arch-card-header">
                <span class="arch-card-title">Final Layer Normalization</span>
              </div>
              <span class="arch-card-sub">Normalization After Transformer Stack</span>
              <p class="arch-card-p">
                A final LayerNorm is applied after the encoder and decoder stacks. This complements the Pre-LN design and helps maintain normalized representations before passing them to subsequent output projection components.
              </p>
              <span class="arch-card-ref">Design detail: Stabilizes outputs prior to linear unembedding projection.</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Architecture Evolution Section -->
          <div class="info-narrative-section">
            <div class="narrative-title">Architecture Evolution</div>
            <p class="narrative-p">
              The current architecture was redesigned after early experiments with a conventional Post-LN Transformer showed slow convergence under limited training resources. The model was updated to use Pre-Layer Normalization and a more compact attention and feed-forward configuration, guided by research on Transformer optimization and low-resource neural machine translation.
            </p>
